        {
          "code": "A1",
          "desc": "Applying the chain rule to obtain $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}$"
        }
      ],
      "solution": "$y = \\frac{x+3}{6-x}$\n$\\therefore \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{(1)(6-x) - (-1)(x+3)}{(6-x)^2} \\quad \\{\\text{quotient rule}\\}$\n$= \\frac{6-x+x+3}{(6-x)^2}$\n$= \\frac{9}{(6-x)^2}$\n$= 9(6-x)^{-2}$\n$\\therefore \\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = -18(6-x)^{-3} \\times (-1) \\quad \\{\\text{chain rule}\\}$\n$= \\frac{18}{(6-x)^3}$",
      "ai_explanation": {
        "concept_rule": "Quotient rule followed by chain rule for second derivative.",
        "step_by_step": [
          "Apply quotient rule: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1\\cdot(6-x) - (x+3)(-1)}{(6-x)^2}$.",
          "Simplify numerator: $6-x+x+3 = 9$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 9(6-x)^{-2}$.",
          "Differentiate again using chain rule: $9(-2)(6-x)^{-3}(-1) = 18(6-x)^{-3}$.",
          "Write as fraction: $\\frac{18}{(6-x)^3}$."
        ],
        "common_pitfalls": "Forgetting the derivative of $(6-x)$ with respect to $x$ is $-1$."
      }
    }
  ]
